% =======================================================
\section{Continuous Magnetic Hard-Iron Correction}
\label{sec:mag-hard-iron}

The startup alignment of Sec.~\ref{sec:mag-two-stage} fixes the yaw gauge from a
magnetic world reference; a body-fixed magnetometer offset therefore biases yaw
even with stable attitude tracking.  The measurement-only estimator
continuously updates the additive magnetic offset without writing attitude state
or resetting Kalman covariance.

\subsection{Why the standing error is a gauge error}
\label{sec:mag-gauge}

Let $\mat A_i$ be the yaw-stripped body-to-level rotation from the
Mahony proxy, $\vct B_\ell$ the level-frame field, and $\vct b_m$ a body-fixed
additive offset.  The raw measurement is
\begin{equation}
\vct m_i=\mat A_i^{\top}\vct B_\ell+\vct b_m+\vct n_i .
\label{eq:mag-offset-model}
\end{equation}
Startup averaging gives
\begin{equation}
\bar{\vct w}
=\frac{1}{N}\sum_i\mat A_i\vct m_i
=\vct B_\ell+\bar{\mat A}\vct b_m,
\qquad
\bar{\mat A}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_i\mat A_i .
\label{eq:mag-ref-average}
\end{equation}
At nearly constant heading, $\bar{\mat A}\approx\mat I$, so the learned
reference follows $\vct B_\ell+\vct b_m$ rather than the true field.  Once this
direction defines north, internally consistent attitude updates preserve the
gauge error; longer averaging reduces noise but cannot separate field and
offset at one attitude.

Eliminating $\vct B_\ell$ gives
\begin{equation}
\underbrace{(\mat I-\bar{\mat A}^{\top}\bar{\mat A})}_{\mat M}\vct b_m
=\bar{\vct m}-\bar{\mat A}^{\top}\bar{\vct w} .
\label{eq:mag-normal}
\end{equation}
Small roll/pitch excitation makes $\mat M$ nonzero but poorly conditioned; the
limitation is excitation geometry rather than sample count, motivating
continued estimation after startup.

\subsection{Exogenous continuous offset estimator}
\label{sec:mag-hi-estimator}

Exponentially weighted statistics of $(\mat A_i,\vct m_i)$ use an effective
\SI{600}{s} memory, with offset updates once per second.  Rotations come from
the yaw-stripped proxy and magnetic input is raw, before offset subtraction, so
neither MEKF state nor corrected measurements feed back into the fit.

Because Eq.~\eqref{eq:mag-offset-model} omits scale, cross-axis, and misalignment
errors, weak excitation can alias them into the hard-iron estimate.  The solve
therefore uses an excitation-scaled ridge,
\begin{equation}
\left[\mat M+\left(\rho_0+\rho_1\frac{\operatorname{tr}\mat M}{3}\right)
\mat I\right]\hat{\vct b}_m
=\bar{\vct m}-\bar{\mat A}^{\top}\bar{\vct w},
\label{eq:mag-ridge}
\end{equation}
with $\rho_0=4\times10^{-3}$ and $\rho_1=0.5$.  The excitation-dependent term
limits misspecification when larger motion increases both geometric information
and unmodeled distortion.

An estimate is accepted only after at least \num{500} effective samples, with
$N\lambda_{\min}(\mat M)\ge2$, fitted offset below \pct{35} of field norm, and
level-frame residual no greater than \SI{3}{\micro\tesla} RMS.  These gates
reject insufficient excitation and gross model failure but cannot identify
soft-iron or misalignment components that the additive model can fit.

\subsection{Application without an attitude reset}
\label{sec:mag-hi-apply}

Subtracting the same offset from measurement and learned reference would leave
the existing innovation, and hence the yaw error, unchanged.  Instead, offset
subtraction is slewed into the raw magnetometer stream with a \SI{45}{s} time
constant while the acquired reference direction is retained; only its magnitude
and dip are adjusted.  The ordinary magnetometer update then corrects yaw.

No attitude state or covariance reset is applied.  After startup, the correction
is a bounded measurement-model change within the normal Live recursion.  If the
offset estimator never validates, measurement and reference remain unchanged.

\subsection{Limitations of the additive model}
\label{sec:mag-hi-limits}

The model identifies only a body-fixed additive offset.  Fixed-heading records
cannot generally separate hard iron from soft-iron scale/cross-axis error or
sensor misalignment, and aliased distortions may still satisfy the residual
gate.  Full distortion identification requires multi-heading excitation; the
current implementation instead stands down when turning violates its residual
assumptions.

Additional calibration draws include misalignment-dominated cases in which the
additive correction degrades performance, despite strong offset dominance in
the reported draw.  The ridge in Eq.~\eqref{eq:mag-ridge} therefore limits model
misspecification as well as numerical ill-conditioning.  Section~\ref{sec:mag-hi-results}
reports the deterministic result for the evaluated draw.
